annotations/898e7135.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nInflate a frame's hole-layout and fill each hole with its matching piece.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Largest component is a monochrome frame enclosing bg holes.\n- Outside pieces composed of k×k blocks; k = side of smallest solid square piece.\n\nRule:\n- Extract connected hole regions inside the frame as slots.\n- Match each piece to a slot by comparing block-level shapes under rotation/flip.\n- Output the layout scaled by k, with pieces placed in slots; frame color as background.\n\nKey insight: Pieces are k×k-block-scaled versions of holes; downsampling reveals shape matches under all 8 orientations.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"sprite s outside the rectangle with holes\",\n  \"dst\": \"fill hole d in the rectangle with holes\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.shape matches enlarged_d.shape up to rotation/flip\",\n  \"transform\": \"enlarge the rectangle to let s fit into its hole\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"crop to only retain the enlarged rectangle\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3cd86f4f\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3096\nmechanism=0.3920\nlexical=0.0154\ncode_feature=0.3333\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:rotation, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:reflect, output:same_size, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/3cd86f4f.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/3cd86f4f.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nShear the rectangular input down-left onto a black canvas.\n\nInput structure\nA rectangular grid of colored pixels with no internal empty region to infer.\n\nRule\nCreate a black output with the same height and width W + H - 1, then copy\neach input row r unchanged starting at column H - 1 - r.\n\nKey insight\nThe colors and row contents are preserved; only the row offsets change so\nthe left edge forms a descending diagonal.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"the full input rectangle, interpreted as rows to be diagonally staggered\",\n  \"dst\": \"black canvas with the same height and enough columns for the staggered rows\",\n  \"transform\": \"copy every row unchanged; place row r starting at column H - 1 - r so adjacent rows shift one column left downward\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"same height, width W + H - 1\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a25697e4\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3574\nmechanism=0.4557\nlexical=0.0127\ncode_feature=0.3750\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:counter, code:rotation, object:frame, object:hole, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:reflect, operation:rotate, operation:shape_match, relation:inside\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/a25697e4.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/a25697e4.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nDock movable tools into border-anchored frames by filling cavities.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Border-touching components cluster into anchor frames with interior bg cavities.\n- Non-border 2-color components are movable tools.\n\nRule:\n- Match each tool to a frame whose cavity size equals one of its color-region counts.\n- Rotate/flip the tool so one color fills the cavity exactly; the other stays outside.\n- If the docking orientation uses a reflection, swap the tool's two colors.\n\nKey insight: Cavity size determines tool–frame pairing; reflection triggers color swap.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"sprite s not touching the edges\",\n  \"dst\": \"dock into frames d on edge\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.component.shape == d.notch.shape with rotation/flip\",\n  \"transform\": \"swap colors of the two components of s if flip is used\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"62593bfd\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3481\nmechanism=0.3907\nlexical=0.0123\ncode_feature=0.6667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:counter, code:rotation, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:reflect, operation:rotate, operation:shape_match, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/62593bfd.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/62593bfd.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSort objects to top or bottom edge by whether their shape (up to rotation/flip) is shared.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several multicolor connected components (8-connected) scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Compute each object's canonical shape (binary mask, invariant to rotation and flip).\n- Objects whose shape appears in two or more components → translate to TOP edge (row 0).\n- Objects whose shape is unique → translate to BOTTOM edge (row H−h).\n- Horizontal position (left column) is preserved; only vertical translation changes.\n\nKey insight: The sorting criterion is shape frequency (rotation-invariant), not area — duplicated shapes go up, unique shapes go down.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"all connected components (objects) on the background\",\n  \"dst\": \"translate each object d vertically to an edge (x preserved)\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"group objects by their pixel-count (area). If an area value occurs >=2 times, those objects go to the TOP edge; if the area is unique, that object goes to the BOTTOM edge\",\n  \"transform\": \"duplicated-size -> top; singleton-size -> bottom\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"cf5fd0ad\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3049\nmechanism=0.4136\nlexical=0.0184\ncode_feature=0.1667\noverlap_tags=code:rotation, object:hole, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:reflect, operation:rotate, output:same_size, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/cf5fd0ad.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/cf5fd0ad.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nBuild a four-quadrant rotational tiling of the input square.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a square grid treated as one complete sprite.\n\nRule\n- Each output quadrant is a 2x2 repetition of a rotated copy of the whole input.\n- The quadrant rotations are upper-left 180 degrees, upper-right 90 degrees\n  clockwise, lower-left 90 degrees counterclockwise, and lower-right unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The output is a 4x4 grid of original-size blocks, not a per-pixel scale-up.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"entire square input grid used as a sprite\",\n  \"dst\": \"4x4 grid of original-size blocks\",\n  \"transform\": \"fill each 2x2 quadrant of blocks with one repeated rotation: upper-left 180 degrees, upper-right 90 degrees clockwise, lower-left 90 degrees counterclockwise, lower-right unchanged\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"input height and width multiplied by 4\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2ccd9fef\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2918\nmechanism=0.3500\nlexical=0.0121\ncode_feature=0.4286\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:rotation, object:frame, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:reflect, operation:shape_match\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/2ccd9fef.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/2ccd9fef.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nPredict the next panel in a framed sequence by continuing each scheduled colored interval.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is made from three or more equal panels in one row or one column.\n- The final input panel is the static target template; every earlier panel differs from it only by dynamic colors absent from the template.\n- Each dynamic color appears in a regular temporal schedule across the earlier panels.\n\nRule\n- Keep the final template panel as the output canvas.\n- For every dynamic color whose schedule reaches the next panel, compare its last two appearances.\n- Extrapolate each one-dimensional interval along the color's changing row or column axis and paint that predicted mask.\n\nKey insight\n- The task is not asking for the last visible panel, but for the next frame after the blank/template slot.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"sequence of equal rectangular panels ending with a blank/static template panel\",\n  \"dst\": \"next panel in the sequence\",\n  \"transform\": \"use the template panel as the output canvas; for each dynamic color absent from the template, follow its regular panel schedule and extrapolate the last two interval changes one more step along the changing row or column axis\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"same shape as one panel\"\n}\n</annotation>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
